# Backward compatibility: none until `8.0.0`, then ADR 329

## Until `8.0.0`

Every release is a release candidate and may break. Change the implementation directly. Unless a decision record requires it (ADR 257 keeps a renamed PSL name as a deprecated alias, with tests), do NOT add:
- Backward compatibility code, aliases or deprecation warnings
- Comments explaining "legacy" or "old" vs "new" approaches
- Tests, fixtures or examples that keep an old pattern beside the new one

Remove the old code, update every reference, test and design doc, and record upgrade instructions (`record-upgrade-instructions` skill).

## From `8.0.0`

[ADR 329](../../docs/architecture%20docs/adrs/ADR%20329%20-%20From%208.0.0%2C%20an%20application%20upgrades%20within%20Prisma%208%20without%20changes.md) applies to everything Generally Available:
- Nothing Generally Available is removed or changed incompatibly within a major. A replaced API stays, deprecated, until the next major.
- Specified behaviour (what the reference documentation states) changes only in a new major, as a new API the user adopts, or through a security fix with a published notice. A fix to wrong behaviour and a change to unspecified detail, such as SQL text, may ship in any release. State the class of your change.
- A later release reads every stored file and database record an earlier release wrote, and the same stored content keeps the same hash.
- Code no published package exports (if GA behaviour built on it stays the same), and anything below GA ([ADR 330](../../docs/architecture%20docs/adrs/ADR%20330%20-%20A%20maturity%20level%20is%20written%20in%20a%20package's%20version%20and%20an%20API's%20name.md)) still changes directly, as above.
